\begin{note}
The default \lstinline{__eq__} for objects is identity:
two distinct objects are never equal.
The \lstinline{is} operator tests that identity directly---it is \lstinline{True}
only when both operands are the same object---while \lstinline{==} dispatches to
\lstinline{__eq__} and asks whether the objects are \emph{equal}.
The two agree when \lstinline{__eq__} is the default, but they are never
interchangeable: prefer \lstinline{x is None} to \lstinline{x == None} because
\lstinline{None} is a singleton, and beware that small ints and string literals
are interned, so \lstinline{256 is 256} is \lstinline{True} while
\lstinline{[] is []} is \lstinline{False}.
Python also gives every object a default \lstinline{__hash__} based on its identity.
There is a contract between \lstinline{__eq__} and \lstinline{__hash__}:
if two objects are equal, they must have the same hash.
When you override \lstinline{__eq__},
python automatically sets \lstinline{__hash__ = None},
which makes the class unhashable.
This is to prevent the class from silently violating the contract.
\end{note}
